"""Bounded source witnesses for the comparison table; not an exhaustive answer key."""
WITNESSES = {}
def witness(key, title, fact, record, links):
    WITNESSES[key] = dict(title=title, fact=fact, record=record, links=links)

witness('S01', '#127: membership clears immediately; release now joins cleanup',
    'Six per-service handles keep isolated key spaces. has/get preserves cached undefined and exact factory values. Insertion follows a successful synchronous return: a thrown factory is retried, while a returned rejected promise stays cached. Clearing removes entries without closing handles or cancelling old work. Successful refresh supplies the file array to beginTurn; failed refresh never reaches it. The service now clears and awaits the project graph, and the backend awaits the service. Generic graph release is sequential and can stop at a rejecting project; the concrete TypeScript cleanup is synchronous. The old five service cases and helpers are byte-identical; six service and four core cases are added.',
    'Stated: the PR’s diagram, public Promise signature and specifying commit disclose the await. The Decisions section gives the clear-before-await reason. Unexplained in this record: reconciliation with broad failure preservation, separate files-only API and lifetime-minimalism choices. No Symnav tests were executed by this critique.',
    [('pr-127-diff',1,'Backend call-site delta'),('pr-127-diff',304,'Turn boundary and service implementation'),('pr-127-diff',458,'Core identity/failure characterizations and implementation'),('pr-127-body',22,'Awaited boundary in author diagram'),('pr-127-body',76,'Reasons and specifying commit'),('127-tests-base',1,'Prior service tests'),('127-tests-head',1,'Head service tests')])
witness('S02', '#131: a new failure location before Worker construction',
    'The daemon-side constructor calls DaemonPolicy.fromSerialized on the complete serialized snapshot to obtain its chunk cap, before new Worker. Malformed policy can therefore throw synchronously in that constructor before allocation. Parsing inside the worker was already present. This is a static control-flow consequence, not a constructor run performed here.',
    'Stated at family level: consumers receive centralized validated policy. Unexplained in the inspected #131 author record: a separate reason for adding this parent failure boundary.',
    [('131-worker',72,'Parse at line 80, Worker at line 86'),('pr-131-diff',1168,'Runtime import and new constructor parse')])
witness('S03', '#131: adapters change precedence and storage premises',
    'Controller/startup helpers prefer a supplied policy over old numeric knobs. The workspace helper takes supplied policy as its base, then applies legacy overrides; memoryCapBytes is accepted but never read. The transport helper clamps inline zero to the default 64 KiB chunk cap and creates the requested directory. In the corrupt-resume fixture, four record-N lines total 36 raw bytes, so the client can stay inline; an empty directory after rejection need not witness deletion of a spill file. Other spool fixtures deliberately cross a positive threshold, and one capture comparison changes inline 1 to 32. These are different translations. Large-output tests remain.',
    'Stated: keep permissive test construction outside required production inputs. Unexplained in the inspected author record: the separate precedence rules, ignored knob and each storage-witness tradeoff. Validated positive and ordered limits explain what the helper must satisfy, not why this exact translation was chosen.',
    [('pr-131-diff',3251,'Controller policy precedence'),('pr-131-diff',3455,'Startup policy precedence'),('pr-131-diff',3552,'Transport floor and directory creation'),('pr-131-diff',3681,'Workspace base, overrides and unused memory knob'),('131-transport-tests',505,'Four tiny records'),('131-transport-tests',582,'Inline-zero call and empty-directory assertion'),('pr-131-diff',1,'Capture fixture threshold translation')])
witness('S04', '#131: required policy width is not constructor validation',
    'The selected resource supervisor receives all nine resources fields but reads six; the terminator receives all eight shutdown fields but reads two; the registry receives all six startup fields but reads one leaf at two sites. Their constructors do not add whole-policy validation. The registry’s startupOwnerIsWithinGrace still allows a numeric override and uses an inclusive boundary. StoredCommandOutput keeps an optional maximumRecordBytes with a non-null assertion on the file-reading path. Transport writeChunkSize, clocks and storage are separate collaborator seams.',
    'Stated: centralized authority and required consumer inputs. Unexplained in the inspected record: the exact broad section widths and retained optional scalar choices. They do not imply arbitrary production threshold reconstruction.',
    [('pr-131-diff',1624,'Resources constructor and reads'),('pr-131-diff',1206,'Terminator policy reads'),('pr-131-diff',1258,'Registry constructor and grace seam'),('pr-131-diff',154,'Optional stored-output record capacity')])
witness('S05', '#131 recovery is not the tip recovery algorithm',
    'The outer numeric loop awaits a reattached completion on its next iteration. A later corrupt completion can therefore escape, while the base’s inner reattach try/catch rethrew the first close. If obtaining the new execute receipt itself fails, #131 still throws the preceding error. Fetch allowance is local to executeOnce, but fetch failure calls fail; increasing its numeric limit does not create repeated fetch attempts here. The added scenario exercises two executes and one fetch on the reattached attempt, not a fetch on both attempts.',
    'Stated: policy owns independent allowances. Unexplained in the inspected #131 record: changed error selection and numeric fetch generality without a repeated-fetch loop. The stack tip is inspected separately in S16.',
    [('pr-131-diff',2688,'Outer error-selection delta'),('131-transport',404,'Head outer completion loop'),('131-transport',458,'Inner recovery and terminal catch'),('131-transport-tests',846,'Added reattach-then-fetch scenario'),('131-transport-base',388,'Prior nesting')])
witness('S06', '#131: deleted oracles are not newly recreated central tests',
    'The central policy implementation and tests are byte-identical across #131. The removed local resource suite contains five derivation rows, a constrained-memory case and a constant assertion; the existing central input table is not identical, including no exact 256 MiB row. A behavioral 250 ms consumer check remains. StartupTimeoutMs had initialization but no read, so deleting the 5 ms fixture setting does not remove a working deadline. Selected new consumer checks cast through unknown to inspect private state; the source-name guard checks listed spellings, not all semantic bypasses.',
    'Stated: retire duplicate local derivation and characterize policy consumption. Unexplained: separate justification for each unmatched input/assertion and private-observation choice. This is a statement of evidence boundaries, not a verdict on the tests.',
    [('pr-131-diff',1321,'Resource test delta'),('131-policy-tests',1,'Unchanged central oracle'),('pr-131-diff',1913,'Removed 5 ms setup'),('pr-131-diff',2110,'Unused startup field removed'),('pr-131-diff',3787,'Retired-spelling guard')])
witness('S07', '#131: helper imports change benchmark output',
    'The replacement test resource record includes replacementWindowMs and replacementLimit in addition to its former resource fields. The benchmark assigns .record and includes that record in its returned artifact, while both its type and emitted schemaVersion remain 1. This is a source-derived reporting-shape change; no benchmark was run here.',
    'Unexplained in the inspected #131 author record: a separate reason for expanding the report under the same schema version.',
    [('pr-131-diff',3094,'Benchmark import changes'),('pr-131-diff',3396,'Replacement record fields'),('131-benchmark',135,'Record selected'),('131-benchmark',185,'Version 1 and resourcePolicy emitted')])
witness('S08', '#148: host policy and failed loading belong to client lifetime',
    'The public host contract has policy?: DaemonPolicy. Runtime composition uses that value or DaemonPolicy.currentSystem. The public constructor immediately starts one runtime-loading promise; execute and control await that same promise, including a rejection. It does not automatically reload after failure. Runtime composition retains its registry, coordinator, transports and controllers for the client lifetime. Node-free public declarations do not remove Node requirements from actual runtime execution.',
    'Stated: the host keeps product/executor knowledge and the client hides daemon composition. Unexplained in the inspected record: a separate comparative reason for optional policy/currentSystem, constructor-load timing and rejected-load retention.',
    [('148-contracts',6,'Optional host policy'),('148-runtime',85,'Default supplier and composition'),('148-facade',20,'Dynamic loader'),('148-facade',39,'One promise for all operations')])
witness('S09', '#148: control credentials partition by operation',
    'Identify and terminate/kill dispatch before the normal protocol gate; their handlers require instance and token. The remaining request path checks protocol and instance. Ping and ordinary stop use that path without a token check; execution admission and status/fetch/ACK add token checks. “Control exceptions” alone does not identify which operations require which authority. These characterize retained behavior; this witness does not call them new authentication changes.',
    'Stated at architecture level: retain protocol behavior and authenticate authority before stateful work. The specific operation partition is read from implementation; no separate comparative reason was located for every exception.',
    [('148-coordinator',353,'Dispatch and normal protocol/instance gate'),('148-coordinator',401,'Early credential handlers'),('148-coordinator',480,'Execute admission token check')])
witness('S10', '#148: changed standalone clock, preserved composed clock',
    'The old standalone request queue defaults to Date.now. The head defaults to a daemon clock and records starts through monotonicNowMs. The process already explicitly constructed the old queue with a monotonic callback, so its composed timing source was already monotonic. “Wall timestamps, monotonic durations” is useful orientation but does not disclose that default/composition distinction.',
    'Stated: daemon clock ownership separates wall timestamps from elapsed time. Unexplained in the inspected record: a distinct reason or parity reconciliation for this standalone default change.',
    [('pr-148-diff',1792,'Standalone constructor/default delta'),('pr-146-diff',1440,'Earlier process explicitly supplies monotonic callback'),('tip-queue',32,'Final queue clock contract')])
witness('S11', '#148: canonical equality tightens the live CLI copy',
    'Some base ownership questions compare only instanceId. The common head predicate always checks identityKey and instanceId; more specific questions add credentials, owner kind/PID or the full observed record including timestamps and revision. A readable same-instance, different-identity startup lock can therefore change isStartupOwner/refreshStartupOwner from acceptance to refusal. Controller ownership checks tighten too. These edits happen before the CLI copies are frozen, so copying does not make them behavior-free.',
    'Stated: one owner for lock equality and narrow named registry operations. Unexplained in the inspected record: separate reconciliation of the tightened outcomes with broad preservation intent. The conflicting-identity example follows predicates; it is not a newly executed corruption probe.',
    [('pr-148-diff',1014,'Live CLI registry delta'),('pr-148-diff',77,'Live CLI controller delta'),('148-registry',516,'Named ownership queries'),('148-registry',842,'Common predicate')])
witness('S12', '#148: a retirement condition exists outside the patch',
    'At the exact #148 base, plans/005/daemon-policy.md says Phase 26 removes @symnav/daemon/policy-testing after app-owned mechanism tests move package-local. That pre-existing paragraph supplies a condition for export retirement even though it is not changed in the #148 patch. The export and its special lint checks are removed as helpers move local. This condition does not individually justify all removed assertions or runner choices.',
    'Stated: the located migration condition. Unexplained at the narrower level: separate reasons for each deleted lint/test observation. An absence claim based only on changed lines can miss an unchanged author record.',
    [('148-policy-record-base',66,'Pinned base migration condition'),('pr-148-diff',1902,'Policy helper relocation'),('pr-148-diff',2139,'Lint restriction deletion'),('pr-148-diff',2258,'Export and lint test delta')])
witness('S13', '#148 staging and test migration have different witnesses',
    'The CLI remains live with 38 retained counterparts while the package adds its graph; 12 retained production files are edited before the freeze. The compatibility digest normalizes CRLF and pins source, not behavior. Of 38 matched production counterparts, 28 match after imports and blank lines are removed; all ten residual comparisons were read. Mechanism tests move package-local. The worker→CLI wrong-version case is replaced by a direct factory rejection, and three startup plus four result-duration expectations disappear from the readiness scenario. New built entry tests do not recreate the removed exact 257 MiB mocked policy handoff. Package tests add tsx and serial files. Windows forced-exit cleanup is asserted through the surviving observer, while other platforms expect child cleanup and a termination diagnostic.',
    'Stated: move mechanism ownership, keep package fixtures independent, freeze live CLI copies and prove executable entries. Unexplained in the inspected record: equivalence of every changed test observation, exact runner choices and the platform split. No Windows or Symnav test run was performed here.',
    [('pr-148-diff',2167,'Compatibility freeze'),('pr-148-diff',26,'New direct executor-factory version assertion'),('pr-148-diff',15682,'Moved worker tests and changed assertions'),('pr-148-diff',300,'Deleted mocked entry test'),('148-built-entry-tests',245,'Platform-specific cleanup assertions'),('pr-148-diff',18353,'Serial package test files')])
witness('S14', 'Stack: configured primary selection is last-owner wins',
    'buildOwnership walks configurations in order, appends each owner to the owners list, and unconditionally sets primaryProjectByRelativePath for each file. A later owner overwrites an earlier one. Thus the final primary is the last configured owner containing the file. This supports page 14’s statement and corrects the first-owner statements in 01 and 22.',
    'Implementation establishes selection. No separate reason for choosing last over first was located in this targeted source inspection; this witness resolves the source statement, not the desirability of the choice.',
    [('tip-graph',319,'Ownership list and unconditional primary overwrite')])
witness('S15', 'Stack: publication transactions stop at their owner',
    'TypeScript refresh updates source cache, then project graph for workspace coverage, then revision state. Revision state prepares and validates a candidate, commits backend preparation, then swaps its index; failed preparation/commit runs rollback. These are local boundaries, not a transaction across the earlier source/graph updates. Sequential per-file ensure operations can retain earlier success if a later file fails. Successful semantic invalidation follows state refresh; it does not roll back earlier owners.',
    'Stated by the introduced ownership/transaction contracts: publish consistent owner state. A whole-refresh rollback is not supplied by the inspected composition. The witness describes scope, without inventing a broader guarantee.',
    [('tip-ts-backend',79,'Cross-owner refresh sequence'),('tip-revisions',57,'Candidate, commit, publish, rollback'),('tip-revisions',83,'Sequential ensure operations'),('tip-graph',119,'Graph publication boundary')])
witness('S16', 'Stack tip: retained fetch state and new execute attempts',
    'A fetch gets a new connection/decoder while keeping receiver, capture, manifest identity, digest and next-record ordinal. The ordinal advances after awaited append. Execute reattachment resubmits the same request but creates fresh capture/receiver and inner allowance, retaining the outer count. Unlike #131, tip fetch failure can recursively resume an eligible accepted close. Exhausted incomplete clean fetch EOF becomes accepted corruption. The first accepted close is retained for later eligible-close exhaustion or receipt-setup failure; unrelated corruption escapes itself. After validated finish, ACK precedes public completion, and ACK failure disposes even finished output.',
    'Stated: package-owned recovery, replay safety and capture cleanup. Exact default allowances and every terminal ordering rationale are not separately explained in the inspected records. Read the pinned version: this witness must not be substituted into the #131 row.',
    [('tip-execution-client',47,'Outer error retention and allowance'),('tip-execution-client',76,'Fresh execution capture and repeated fetch handling'),('tip-receiver',59,'Append before ordinal advancement'),('tip-execution-client',208,'Finish, ACK, disposal and public completion'),('tip-execution-client',300,'Fetch connection/decoder')])
witness('S17', 'Accepted execution and delivery keep distinct state owners',
    'Acceptance metadata persists with the daemon-lifetime ledger. Matching duplicates return before another trace, queue turn, clock read or lifetime reset. The queued turn finishes the spool before terminal publication, reads the latest tracked delivery promise once, then waits on that promise; it is not an aggregation of future attachments and is not ordinary ACK. Turn-boundary sampling is scheduled in finally and gates the next FIFO turn; sampling failure is diagnostic. ACK validates transfer, awaits a cleanup attempt, then acknowledges logically even if cleanup failed. Trace expiry removes tracing, not ledger identity or retained spool. Graceful shutdown closes workers before spool cleanup.',
    'Stated explicitly in #146/#147 Decisions: latest stream avoids another registry, cleanup failure must not withhold protocol success, immutable identity avoids replay effects, and sampling must gate the next turn after success or failure. The exact split into two PRs is not separately reasoned there.',
    [('pr-146-body',116,'Delivery design reasons'),('pr-147-body',93,'Acceptance and sampling reasons'),('tip-accepted',45,'Duplicate early return'),('tip-accepted',129,'Finish, publication, captured promise and finally'),('tip-delivery',192,'Cleanup attempt then logical ACK'),('tip-delivery',286,'Latest promise with guarded removal'),('tip-delivery',430,'Trace-only expiration'),('pr-146-diff',1175,'Process delivery and shutdown barrier tests')])
witness('S18', 'Stack cutover: parsed events change diagnostic observation',
    'The read-only inspector reads numbered log backups in descending suffix order and then the active log, parses lines and filters malformed JSON/schema records. Its cursor is a nonnegative safe integer; missing paths are tolerated but other I/O errors propagate. The migrated diagnostic test checks JSON.stringify of parsed events for secrets, replacing a raw-file assertion; it also loses file-count and rotation-size assertions. A malformed line can now be absent from that observation. Ten status/stop CLI scenarios are removed elsewhere in the cutover. None of this equates inspector convenience with equivalent evidence.',
    'Stated: expose public read-only test inspection while enforcing package ownership. Unexplained in the inspected record: scenario-specific removal or a proof that filtered observations replace all prior raw-byte/file assertions.',
    [('tip-inspector',71,'Cursor and observation API'),('tip-inspector',91,'Backup ordering, parse/filter and I/O handling'),('stack-diff',7396,'Diagnostic-output test migration'),('stack-diff',8287,'Status test removals'),('stack-diff',8996,'Stop test removals')])
witness('S19', 'Admission and outer failure classification have ordered authority',
    'Admission evaluates authentication, worker readiness, resource pause, queue state, then request compatibility. The process requests an admission sample without awaiting it and reads the current snapshot. Rejection codes own retrySafe; contradictory supplied booleans fail validation. For accepted execution, outer classification checks per-request resource interruption, capacity, worker exit (except explicit stopping), recorded shutdown, then shutdown-started/internal. A controlled-resource shutdown does not alone reclassify an unmarked worker exit.',
    'Stated: one authoritative admission/failure vocabulary and package-owned replay permission. The source order is the mechanism; a free-standing retry boolean or worker label cannot override it.',
    [('tip-admission',36,'Ordered first-rejection guards'),('tip-admission',91,'Code-owned retry safety'),('148-coordinator',480,'Unawaited admission sampling and snapshot'),('tip-failure',23,'Outer failure precedence')])
witness('S20', 'Dependency arrows and supplied values are separate edges',
    'The #131 code imports DaemonPolicy from @symnav/daemon in CLI composition/helpers and passes policy values into CLI-owned consumers. Thus an arrow labeled imports/constructs should not depict daemon policy importing the CLI. The gray arrow in the 52 variants points the opposite way; the repaired 59 variants reverse it and distinguish dependency from value supply. This is a source/representation correction at the same depth, not a surprise on descent.',
    'The gray-arrow interpretation follows the diagram’s own legend. No author intent beyond that label is inferred, and spacing variants do not constitute independent comprehension evidence.',
    [('pr-131-diff',1171,'CLI runtime import of daemon policy'),('pr-131-diff',3251,'CLI helper imports and constructs a consumer')])
witness('S21', '#148 routing evaluates only the next necessary guard',
    'Routing first distinguishes absent records, unreadable records and observed startup; a starting record wins before a later version check. Observation then checks pong version before pong state, with separate outcomes for missing, read-error and incompatible responses. Removed records are best effort. Disabled execute bypasses routing, and cold return may schedule warm-up independently. Guard evaluation stops after the first route, so skipped reads/probes are part of the decision.',
    'Stated: preserve lazy first-return routing and keep startup independent from the cold result. The page’s narrow routing model is checked against these owners, not against all #148 mechanism decisions.',
    [('148-routing',1,'Ordered guards and first returned route'),('148-dispatcher-base',1,'Prior app dispatcher'),('148-runtime',139,'Disabled execute and route dispatch')])
